description:
Site: crate-jmap-chat-server/src/space.rs:898 (SPACE_READONLY), 933 (METADATA_FIELDS), 951 (STRUCTURAL_KEYS). All three are declared inside the body of the for-loop in handle_space_set's update branch (lines ~890-988).

Current code:

    pub async fn handle_space_set(...) {
        ...
        if let Some(Value::Object(update_map)) = args.remove("update") {
            for (id_str, patch_val) in update_map {
                ...
                const SPACE_READONLY: &[&str] = &["id", "createdAt", ...];    // L898
                ...
                const METADATA_FIELDS: &[&str] = &["name", "description", ...]; // L933
                ...
                const STRUCTURAL_KEYS: &[&str] = &["addRoles", "updateRoles", ...]; // L951
                ...
            }
        }
    }

clippy::pedantic fires items_after_statements on all three. These are not closure-captured locals; they're three module-scope-sized slice constants (7 + 5 + 12 entries respectively) buried deep inside a function that already triggers too_many_lines (363 lines).

What idiomatic Rust does: hoist module-scope constants to module scope. A const that documents 'these are the spec-defined read-only fields of Space' is module-level vocabulary, not a loop-body local. Hoisted:

    // Module top, near the use-declarations
    const SPACE_READONLY: &[&str] = &["id", "createdAt", "memberCount",
        "roles", "members", "categories", "uncategorizedChannelIds"];
    const SPACE_METADATA_FIELDS: &[&str] = &["name", "description", "iconBlobId",
        "isPublic", "isPubliclyPreviewable"];
    const SPACE_STRUCTURAL_KEYS: &[&str] = &["addRoles", "updateRoles", ...];

What goes wrong if left: readability + grep-ability. A reviewer looking for 'what fields of Space are read-only?' has to grep into a 363-line function body to find SPACE_READONLY, instead of reading the module header. Worse, the same patch_val shape is exercised by Space/get's projection logic (which lives elsewhere in space.rs) — and the projection logic doesn't share the constant because the constant is locally scoped. This is the canonical case for hoisting.

Cross-references JMAP-x2gd.31 which is about how these constants are used (.iter().find() vs match); this finding is about where they are declared. They are complementary, not duplicate.

Items_after_statements also fires on 11 other sites in the diff — most are smaller (use-statements, struct decls) and lower-impact. This is the biggest hit.
